WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.291 --> 00:00:03.791
(gentle orchestral music)

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:19.900
- [Tim] An ice instrument has the purity

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:23.153
and the clarity of the heavenly realm.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.010
It's not of the earth in the way

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:27.713
that a wooden instrument would be.

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:32.833
There's so much potential in
ice as an acoustic material.

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:37.023
(classical piano music)

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:48.623
My name is Tim Linhart, and I
am the founder of ice music.

00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:52.960
Ice music is the music that
we make on this orchestra

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:55.350
of ice instruments.

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:59.140
The idea first came to
me about 21 years ago.

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:01.390
I was an ice sculptor for many years.

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:02.950
Decided one of my sculptures I'd like

00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:05.090
to build a giant violin.

00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:06.900
And I had a friend who
was building guitars

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:08.540
and he just asked the question:

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:10.320
Gee, I wonder how that one's gonna sound?

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:14.153
And that question has swallowed
much of the rest of my life.

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:20.090
The instruments are made of white ice,

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:22.560
which is a mixture of snow and water.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.570
It's filled with
snowflakes and air bubbles

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:28.520
and all kind of different things going on.

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:32.040
To make a single instrument
a violin, or viola here,

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:35.990
the ice part takes about five or six days.

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:38.510
I can probably build the
whole orchestra, here,

00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:40.890
in about seven or eight weeks.

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:45.770
We have violins, violas,
cellos, bass, guitars,

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:49.253
mandolins, banjos, drums, and xylophone.

00:01:51.158 --> 00:01:54.120
- [Photographer] Hold, hold, grazie.

00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:58.680
- Ice music is not just classical music

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:00.390
or experimental music,

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:02.863
it's a wide variety of different things.

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:07.900
This winter we'll put
on about 45 concerts.

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:10.450
The concert hall has a specific design,

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:13.770
it's one giant ice cave.

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:17.440
The igloo, right now, is
10 degrees fahrenheit.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:19.530
It's definitely an issue in ice music

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:21.210
that people's fingers get cold.

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:23.850
People play with a variety
of different kinds of gloves.

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:27.210
Some people play barehanded,
but for me the temperature is,

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:28.480
you know, the thing that I really need

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:31.194
to make ice music work.

00:02:31.194 --> 00:02:35.930
(audience clapping and cheering)

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:39.200
So many people just
cannot wrap their brain

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.880
around a piece of ice making
a sound, or a beautiful sound.

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:45.960
When they come in to the ice concert hall,

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:48.370
they're just very, very
pleasantly surprised

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:51.770
and that releases a real,
kind of, a high for people.

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:54.423
And then, wow, it's amazing.

